  WALTER CORWIN: "There's a shallow furrow scored into the dirt thirty paces back from the kitchen door. Ground oyster shell and yellow brimstone."
  [The groundskeeper drops a crate onto the stack with a sharp, hollow hollow clatter.]
  VOICE: trombone_dismissive_short_v2
  THE GROUNDSKEEPER: "Grange orders. Slugs in the rhododendrons."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Slugs don't cross thirty yards of open carriage gravel in November."
  VOICE: trombone_cautious_long_v2
  THE GROUNDSKEEPER: "They don't cross that line either, Officer. That's why it's there.\nNow step back on the gravel path. You're kicking the lime into the grass."
